{"step_index":41,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:53:41Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-23T22:23:41+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-23T22:23:41+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/find-skill-repo/all%20github%20repos/Open-Generative-AI/README.md`\nTotal Lines: 630\nTotal Bytes: 40160\nShowing lines 151 to 300\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n151:   ```bash\r\n152:   sudo apt install ./release/open-generative-ai_*_amd64.deb\r\n153:   ```\r\n154: \r\n155: If AppImage fails to start on older systems, install `libfuse2`:\r\n156: \r\n157: ```bash\r\n158: sudo apt install libfuse2\r\n159: ```\r\n160: \r\n161: #### Ubuntu 24.04+ / AppArmor sandbox restriction\r\n162: \r\n163: Ubuntu 24.04 and later enable a kernel security policy (`apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns`) that blocks Chromium's user-namespace sandbox. If the app fails to start silently or crashes immediately, you have two options:\r\n164: \r\n165: **Option A — Recommended: install the `.deb` instead.**\r\n166: The `.deb` package ships an AppArmor profile that grants the required permission automatically on install with no system-wide changes.\r\n167: \r\n168: **Option B — Temporary system fix (AppImage users):**\r\n169: ```bash\r\n170: sudo sysctl -w kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0\r\n171: ```\r\n172: This lasts until next reboot. To make it permanent:\r\n173: ```bash\r\n174: echo 'kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0' | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/99-userns.conf\r\n175: ```\r\n176: \r\n177: ---\r\n178: \r\n179: Open Generative AI is a free, open-source AI image, video, cinema, and lip sync studio that brings creative workflows to everyone. No content filters, no prompt rejections, no guardrails — just full creative freedom. Powered by [Muapi.ai](https://muapi.ai?utm_source=github&utm_medium=readme&utm_campaign=open-generative-ai), it supports text-to-image, image-to-image, text-to-video, image-to-video, and audio-driven lip sync generation across models like Flux, Nano Banana, Midjourney, Kling, Sora, Veo, Seedream, Infinite Talk, LTX Lipsync, Wan 2.2, and more — all from a sleek, modern interface you can self-host and customize.\r\n180: \r\n181: **Why Open Generative AI instead of other AI Video Platforms?**\r\n182: - **No filters** — no content filters, no nanny guardrails, no prompt rejections\r\n183: - **Free & open-source** — no subscription, no vendor lock-in\r\n184: - **Self-hosted** — your data stays on your machine, full creative control\r\n185: - **200+ models** — text-to-image, image-to-image, text-to-video, image-to-video, lip sync\r\n186: - **Multi-image input** — feed up to 14 reference images into compatible models\r\n187: - **Lip Sync Studio** — animate portraits or sync lips to any audio with 9 dedicated models\r\n188: - **Extensible** — add your own models, modify the UI, build on top of it\r\n189: \r\n190: For a deep dive into the technical architecture and the philosophy behind the \"Infinite Budget\" cinema workflow, see our [comprehensive guide and roadmap](https://medium.com/@anilmatcha/).\r\n191: \r\n192: ## ⚡ Local Model Inference (Desktop App Only)\r\n193: \r\n194: The desktop app supports **two independent local engines**. Pick whichever fits the machine you actually run on:\r\n195: \r\n196: | Engine | What it is | Best for |\r\n197: |---|---|---|\r\n198: | **sd.cpp** (bundled) | C++ engine from [stable-diffusion.cpp](https://github.com/leejet/stable-diffusion.cpp), runs on the same machine as the app. Metal GPU on Apple Silicon, CUDA/Vulkan/ROCm on Linux/Windows. | Image-only models. Works on Mac M-series. |\r\n199: | **Wan2GP** (BYO server) | HTTP client to a user-run [Wan2GP](https://github.com/deepbeepmeep/Wan2GP) server. The server runs Python + PyTorch on a CUDA/ROCm GPU; the desktop app only sends prompts and receives results. | Video models (Wan 2.2, Hunyuan, LTX) and large image models (Flux, Qwen-Image). NVIDIA/AMD GPU required on the *server*; the desktop app itself can run on a Mac. |\r\n200: \r\n201: Both engines share the same UI: open **Settings → Local Models** to configure each.\r\n202: \r\n203: ### Engine 1 — sd.cpp (bundled)\r\n204: \r\n205: | Model | Type | Size | Notes |\r\n206: |---|---|---|---|\r\n207: | **Z-Image Turbo** ⚡ | Diffusion Transformer | 2.5 GB + 2.7 GB aux | 8-step turbo. Heavy on memory. |\r\n208: | **Z-Image Base** ⚡ | Diffusion Transformer | 3.5 GB + 2.7 GB aux | 50-step high-quality. Heavy on memory. |\r\n209: | **Dreamshaper 8** | SD 1.5 | 2.1 GB | 20-step versatile. Lightest tested option on Mac. |\r\n210: | **Realistic Vision v5.1** | SD 1.5 | 2.1 GB | 25-step photorealistic |\r\n211: | **Anything v5** | SD 1.5 | 2.1 GB | 20-step anime/illustration |\r\n212: | **SDXL Base 1.0** | SDXL | 6.9 GB | 30-step high-res |\r\n213: \r\n214: > **Z-Image models** require two shared auxiliary files (downloaded once, shared across both models):\r\n215: > - **Qwen3-4B Text Encoder** — 2.4 GB\r\n216: > - **FLUX VAE** — 335 MB\r\n217: \r\n218: **How to use:**\r\n219: 1. Open **Settings → Local Models** in the desktop app\r\n220: 2. Install the **sd.cpp inference engine** (one click — auto-downloaded)\r\n221: 3. Download your chosen model (and auxiliary files for Z-Image)\r\n222: 4. In **Image Studio**, click the **⚡ Local** toggle next to the model selector\r\n223: 5. Select your local model and generate — no API key needed\r\n224: \r\n225: All downloads happen inside the app. Nothing is installed system-wide.\r\n226: \r\n227: By default, `sd.cpp` stores the engine, model weights, and temporary downloads under Electron's app data directory. Common paths are:\r\n228: \r\n229: - macOS: `~/Library/Application Support/open-generative-ai/local-ai`\r\n230: - Windows: `%APPDATA%\\open-generative-ai\\local-ai`\r\n231: - Linux: `~/.config/open-generative-ai/local-ai`\r\n232: \r\n233: To keep multi-GB model weights on another drive, set `OPEN_GENERATIVE_AI_LOCAL_AI_DIR`\r\n234: before launching the desktop app. The app will create `bin/`, `models/`, and `tmp/`\r\n235: inside that directory, and **Settings -> Local Models** shows the resolved model folder.\r\n236: Local engine output and download errors are written to the app process console, so launch\r\n237: from Terminal or PowerShell when you need troubleshooting logs.\r\n238: \r\n239: ### Engine 2 — Wan2GP (remote Gradio server)\r\n240: \r\n241: The app does **not** bundle Python or model weights for Wan2GP. You run Wan2GP yourself on a machine with a CUDA or ROCm GPU and point the desktop app at its URL.\r\n242: \r\n243: ```bash\r\n244: # On your GPU machine\r\n245: git clone https://github.com/deepbeepmeep/Wan2GP\r\n246: cd Wan2GP\r\n247: ./install.sh                          # or install.bat on Windows\r\n248: python wgp.py --listen --server-name 0.0.0.0   # binds to all interfaces\r\n249: ```\r\n250: \r\n251: Then in the desktop app: **Settings → Local Models → Wan2GP server**, paste the URL (e.g. `http://192.168.1.42:7860`), click **Test**, then **Save**. The Wan2GP models become available — image models in **Image Studio**, video models reachable via the same generation API (Image Studio rejects video output explicitly; full Video Studio wiring is on the roadmap).\r\n252: \r\n253: | Model | Type | Notes |\r\n254: |---|---|---|\r\n255: | **Flux.1 Dev** | Image | 1024px, 28 steps |\r\n256: | **Qwen Image** | Image | 1024px, 30 steps |\r\n257: | **Wan 2.2 (T2V / I2V)** | Video | Slow on consumer GPUs |\r\n258: | **Hunyuan Video** | Video | High-quality T2V |\r\n259: | **LTX Video** | Video | Fastest video option |\r\n260: \r\n261: > **Why a separate server?** Wan2GP's runtime (Sage attention, flash-attn, AWQ/GGUF kernels) is CUDA-only — there is no MPS / Apple Silicon path. Treating it as a remote server lets a Mac-only user keep the desktop app while offloading inference to a Linux/Windows GPU box, a gaming PC on the LAN, or a rented RunPod/vast.ai instance.\r\n262: \r\n263: > **Local inference is only available in the desktop app.** The hosted web version always uses cloud APIs.\r\n264: \r\n265: ### Hardware Notes\r\n266: \r\n267: - **sd.cpp** runs on CPU (all platforms) and **Metal GPU** on Apple Silicon (M1/M2/M3/M4); CUDA/Vulkan/ROCm on Linux/Windows.\r\n268: - Metal GPU acceleration is built into the macOS desktop binary — significantly faster than CPU-only.\r\n269: - Recommended for sd.cpp Z-Image: 16 GB RAM (7.4 GB weights + 2.4 GB compute buffer). On a base 8 GB M-series Mac, **Z-Image is known to hang the system** — stick to SD 1.5 there.\r\n270: - For SD 1.5 on M2: expect ~1–2 s/step with the Metal dylib active. If you see ~10 s/step instead, the binary may have fallen back to CPU — see verification below.\r\n271: \r\n272: ### Verifying the SD 1.5 path (the fastest sanity test on Mac)\r\n273: \r\n274: If you want to confirm sd.cpp is installed correctly without going through the UI, you can drive `sd-cli` directly. This is the same binary the app uses.\r\n275: \r\n276: ```bash\r\n277: # 1. App data layout (created on first app launch)\r\n278: APP_DATA=\"${OPEN_GENERATIVE_AI_LOCAL_AI_DIR:-$HOME/Library/Application Support/open-generative-ai/local-ai}\"\r\n279: ls \"$APP_DATA/bin\"     # sd-cli, libstable-diffusion.dylib\r\n280: ls \"$APP_DATA/models\"  # whatever you've downloaded\r\n281: \r\n282: # 2. Grab a small SD 1.5 model directly (Dreamshaper 8, ~2 GB)\r\n283: curl -L --fail --progress-bar \\\r\n284:   -o \"$APP_DATA/models/DreamShaper_8_pruned.safetensors\" \\\r\n285:   \"https://huggingface.co/Lykon/DreamShaper/resolve/main/DreamShaper_8_pruned.safetensors\"\r\n286: \r\n287: # 3. Run a single 512x512 / 12-step inference\r\n288: DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH=\"$APP_DATA/bin\" \"$APP_DATA/bin/sd-cli\" \\\r\n289:   -m \"$APP_DATA/models/DreamShaper_8_pruned.safetensors\" \\\r\n290:   -p \"a serene mountain lake at sunrise, oil painting\" \\\r\n291:   -o /tmp/sd15-test.png \\\r\n292:   --steps 12 -H 512 -W 512 --cfg-scale 7.5 --seed 42 \\\r\n293:   --sampling-method euler_a\r\n294: ```\r\n295: \r\n296: A healthy run on Apple Silicon prints `total params memory size = 1969.78MB (VRAM 1969.78MB, RAM 0.00MB)` (Metal-backed) and produces a coherent 512×512 PNG. If `VRAM` is `0.00MB` instead, the dylib is CPU-only — check `otool -L \"$APP_DATA/bin/libstable-diffusion.dylib\" | grep -i metal` and reinstall the engine from **Settings → Local Models** if Metal is missing.\r\n297: \r\n298: ---\r\n299: \r\n300: ## ✨ Features\r\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
